1
00:00:00,950 --> 00:00:03,405
Inside a modern smartphone are sixty-two

2
00:00:03,425 --> 00:00:06,055
microchips, holding about ninety billion

3
00:00:06,075 --> 00:00:07,942
transistors between them.

4
00:00:07,962 --> 00:00:10,542
Each transistor is a switch so small that

5
00:00:10,562 --> 00:00:12,568
more than a thousand would fit side by

6
00:00:12,588 --> 00:00:15,025
side across a single human hair.

7
00:00:15,050 --> 00:00:16,605
None of them were assembled.

8
00:00:16,625 --> 00:00:18,380
They were printed onto silicon, with

9
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:19,093
light.

10
00:00:19,113 --> 00:00:20,175
Here's how.

11
00:00:21,300 --> 00:00:23,380
Open a desktop computer and lift the lid

12
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:24,805
off its processor.

13
00:00:24,825 --> 00:00:26,955
Underneath is a silicon die smaller than a

14
00:00:26,975 --> 00:00:29,405
postage stamp, carrying twenty-six billion

15
00:00:29,425 --> 00:00:31,680
transistors, organized into twenty-four

16
00:00:31,700 --> 00:00:32,643
cores.

17
00:00:32,663 --> 00:00:34,693
Dive into one core, and you fall through a

18
00:00:34,713 --> 00:00:37,105
city of copper wiring, seventeen layers

19
00:00:37,125 --> 00:00:37,930
deep.

20
00:00:37,950 --> 00:00:40,230
Fine local wires at the bottom, wide

21
00:00:40,250 --> 00:00:41,980
global highways at the top.

22
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:43,955
And beneath it all are the transistors

23
00:00:43,975 --> 00:00:45,018
themselves:

24
00:00:45,038 --> 00:00:48,075
fins of silicon just six nanometers wide.

25
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:51,292
To build that, a factory stacks about

26
00:00:51,312 --> 00:00:53,855
eighty patterned layers, each one aligned

27
00:00:53,875 --> 00:00:55,455
to the layer beneath it within a few

28
00:00:55,475 --> 00:00:56,742
nanometers.

29
00:00:56,762 --> 00:00:58,967
The recipe runs to roughly nine hundred

30
00:00:58,987 --> 00:01:01,817
and forty steps, and takes three months.

31
00:01:01,837 --> 00:01:04,217
A single speck of dust in the wrong place

32
00:01:04,237 --> 00:01:05,625
can ruin a chip.

33
00:01:06,750 --> 00:01:08,767
Yet almost every layer follows the same

34
00:01:08,787 --> 00:01:10,005
short recipe.

35
00:01:10,025 --> 00:01:11,730
First, coat the wafer in a thin,

36
00:01:11,750 --> 00:01:14,430
insulating film of silicon dioxide.

37
00:01:14,450 --> 00:01:16,342
Spin on a light-sensitive coating, called

38
00:01:16,362 --> 00:01:17,580
photoresist.

39
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:19,555
Shine ultraviolet light through a stencil,

40
00:01:19,575 --> 00:01:20,742
the photomask.

41
00:01:20,762 --> 00:01:22,455
Wash away the resist the light touched,

42
00:01:22,475 --> 00:01:23,617
leaving a pattern.

43
00:01:23,637 --> 00:01:25,892
Etch that pattern down into the oxide.

44
00:01:25,912 --> 00:01:27,842
Strip off the leftover resist.

45
00:01:27,862 --> 00:01:29,617
Flood the trenches with copper.

46
00:01:29,637 --> 00:01:31,642
Then polish the surface perfectly flat,

47
00:01:31,662 --> 00:01:33,955
until only copper wires, set in glass,

48
00:01:33,975 --> 00:01:34,900
remain.

49
00:01:34,925 --> 00:01:36,300
One layer, done.

50
00:01:36,700 --> 00:01:38,625
Swap the mask, and go again.

51
00:01:39,775 --> 00:01:41,655
Draw that recipe as a loop, and six

52
00:01:41,675 --> 00:01:43,418
families of machines appear.

53
00:01:43,438 --> 00:01:44,992
Lithography patterns.

54
00:01:45,012 --> 00:01:47,042
Deposition adds material.

55
00:01:47,062 --> 00:01:49,300
Etching and polishing take it away.

56
00:01:49,325 --> 00:01:52,380
Ion implanters modify the silicon itself.

57
00:01:52,400 --> 00:01:54,580
Washers clean, and metrology tools

58
00:01:54,600 --> 00:01:55,592
inspect.

59
00:01:55,612 --> 00:01:57,642
Go around the loop eighty times, and a

60
00:01:57,662 --> 00:02:00,325
wafer has made close to a thousand stops.

61
00:02:01,450 --> 00:02:03,542
Those stops happen inside a fab:

62
00:02:03,562 --> 00:02:05,580
a clean room as large as eight football

63
00:02:05,600 --> 00:02:08,105
fields, filled with hundreds of machines,

64
00:02:08,125 --> 00:02:10,055
some costing a hundred and seventy million

65
00:02:10,075 --> 00:02:11,350
dollars each.

66
00:02:11,400 --> 00:02:14,142
Wafers, three hundred millimeters across,

67
00:02:14,162 --> 00:02:16,418
ride in sealed pods of twenty-five,

68
00:02:16,438 --> 00:02:18,568
carried on overhead rails from tool to

69
00:02:18,588 --> 00:02:19,505
tool.

70
00:02:19,525 --> 00:02:21,443
With four hundred and thirty-five tools on

71
00:02:21,463 --> 00:02:23,668
the floor, this fab finishes fifty

72
00:02:23,688 --> 00:02:25,350
thousand wafers a month.

73
00:02:26,475 --> 00:02:29,092
The most important stop is lithography.

74
00:02:29,112 --> 00:02:30,705
Ultraviolet light shines through the

75
00:02:30,725 --> 00:02:33,130
photomask, then through lenses that shrink

76
00:02:33,150 --> 00:02:34,943
the image four times.

77
00:02:34,963 --> 00:02:37,630
The wafer steps beneath the beam, exposure

78
00:02:37,650 --> 00:02:40,155
after exposure, until all two hundred and

79
00:02:40,175 --> 00:02:42,292
thirty chips carry the pattern.

80
00:02:42,312 --> 00:02:44,330
A single processor needs eighty different

81
00:02:44,350 --> 00:02:46,930
masks, each costing around three hundred

82
00:02:46,950 --> 00:02:48,250
thousand dollars.

83
00:02:49,400 --> 00:02:51,455
Deposition tools lay down metals,

84
00:02:51,475 --> 00:02:53,805
insulators, and crystalline silicon, a

85
00:02:53,825 --> 00:02:56,267
little like spray paint through a stencil.

86
00:02:56,287 --> 00:02:58,193
Plasma etchers carve straight down through

87
00:02:58,213 --> 00:03:00,517
whatever the mask leaves exposed.

88
00:03:00,537 --> 00:03:02,367
Chemical mechanical polishing grinds the

89
00:03:02,387 --> 00:03:05,205
surface flat, ready for the next layer.

90
00:03:05,225 --> 00:03:07,492
And at the very bottom, ion implanters

91
00:03:07,512 --> 00:03:09,780
fire boron or phosphorus deep into the

92
00:03:09,800 --> 00:03:12,380
silicon, roughly one atom for every ten

93
00:03:12,400 --> 00:03:14,555
thousand, to create the regions that let

94
00:03:14,575 --> 00:03:16,100
transistors switch.

95
00:03:16,475 --> 00:03:18,580
A quick bake, called an anneal, heals the

96
00:03:18,600 --> 00:03:19,950
crystal afterward.

97
00:03:21,050 --> 00:03:23,292
Between steps, wafers are rinsed in

98
00:03:23,312 --> 00:03:26,375
ultra-pure water and dried with nitrogen.

99
00:03:26,400 --> 00:03:29,255
Electron microscopes scan them for flaws,

100
00:03:29,275 --> 00:03:31,417
because a defect caught today is far

101
00:03:31,437 --> 00:03:33,305
cheaper than one discovered three months

102
00:03:33,325 --> 00:03:34,225
from now.

103
00:03:35,300 --> 00:03:37,280
The wafers themselves begin as quartz

104
00:03:37,300 --> 00:03:40,017
rock, refined into ultra-pure silicon and

105
00:03:40,037 --> 00:03:43,017
grown into a single, flawless crystal.

106
00:03:43,037 --> 00:03:45,280
That crystal is sliced into discs less

107
00:03:45,300 --> 00:03:47,630
than a millimeter thick, each with a tiny

108
00:03:47,650 --> 00:03:50,067
notch marking its crystal direction.

109
00:03:50,087 --> 00:03:52,080
A blank wafer costs about a hundred

110
00:03:52,100 --> 00:03:53,105
dollars.

111
00:03:53,125 --> 00:03:55,205
Finished, it's worth around a hundred

112
00:03:55,225 --> 00:03:56,175
thousand.

113
00:03:57,325 --> 00:03:59,080
Finally, every chip on the wafer is

114
00:03:59,100 --> 00:03:59,992
tested.

115
00:04:00,012 --> 00:04:01,917
A stray particle can knock out a core or

116
00:04:01,937 --> 00:04:04,080
two, so chips are sorted by what still

117
00:04:04,100 --> 00:04:04,930
works.

118
00:04:04,950 --> 00:04:07,842
Flawless dies become top-tier processors.

119
00:04:07,862 --> 00:04:10,155
Slightly damaged ones are sold as cheaper

120
00:04:10,175 --> 00:04:11,092
models.

121
00:04:11,112 --> 00:04:13,592
Each die is then cut free with a laser,

122
00:04:13,612 --> 00:04:15,680
flipped onto a circuit board, and capped

123
00:04:15,700 --> 00:04:17,475
with a metal heat spreader.

124
00:04:17,525 --> 00:04:19,525
Now it's ready for your computer.

125
00:04:20,650 --> 00:04:21,643
Three months.

126
00:04:21,663 --> 00:04:23,143
A thousand steps.

127
00:04:23,163 --> 00:04:26,143
Eighty layers of light, glass, and copper.

128
00:04:26,163 --> 00:04:28,380
All to build twenty-six billion switches

129
00:04:28,400 --> 00:04:30,455
on a sliver of silicon smaller than a

130
00:04:30,475 --> 00:04:31,425
stamp.

131
00:04:31,475 --> 00:04:33,867
Few things people make are more precise,

132
00:04:33,887 --> 00:04:35,380
and dozens of them are in your pocket

133
00:04:35,400 --> 00:04:36,350
right now.
